# Element-wise arithmetic on two growable [T] arrays of DIFFERENT lengths must
# abort cleanly at runtime, naming BOTH lengths. A [T] length is only known at
# run time, so this is the one mismatch the compiler cannot catch; padding the
# short side or truncating the long one would both be a silent wrong answer.
#
# Expected: exit 1 with
#   tycho: element-wise arithmetic on arrays of different lengths (3 and 2)
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT IN tests/abort/. `scripts/frontparity.sh` globs
# `tests/abort/*.ty` and scores "tychoc accepted it, tychoc0 refused it" as a
# DIVERGENCE. This program is post-freeze: tychoc accepts it and the frozen
# tychoc0 refuses it as a type error, so putting it in tests/abort/ would redden
# frontparity by construction -- the exact trap tests/postfreeze/ exists to
# avoid. tests/reject/ has no such problem (it is not in frontparity's glob at
# all, and tychoc refuses those programs anyway, which frontparity skips), but
# an abort fixture is a program tychoc ACCEPTS, so it needs its own post-freeze
# home. tests/run.sh runs this directory with the same discipline as
# tests/abort/: build with tychoc, run native-only, require a nonzero exit and a
# 'tycho:' message. Native-only because a deliberate exit(1) leaves live arenas
# and LeakSanitizer would report them, correctly and uselessly.

fn main():
    a := [1, 2, 3]
    b := [4, 5]
    println("about to multiply arrays of different lengths")
    println(str(a * b))
